  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nSurface cold front/wind shift has moved through most of the CWA,\nand high pressure should move in behind it for tonight. With\nfairly light winds and only a few cirrus clouds, radiational\ncooling will be good, with the northern counties dropping into\nthe mid 30s and a 50 percent chance for below 32 degrees around\nTowner county. However, temps in that area have already dipped\ninto the upper 20s, so no freeze headlines are needed at this \npoint. Temps will recover quickly as we head into the work week\nand upper ridging builds into the Plains and Upper Midwest.\nA weak shortwave moving over the top of the ridge through Canada\nwill again bring a weak frontal boundary/wind shift through on\nWednesday, but not much impact expected. Temperatures will\nremain above seasonal averages throughout the period with the\nECMWF EFI showing only more of a warm signal as we head into\nnext weekend and flow turns southwesterly.\n\nPattern starts to turn more active towards the end of the work\nweek as flow becomes more southwesterly and a lead shortwave and\nwarm air advection come into the region. Exact timing of the\nupper trough coming out into the Plains is all over the place\nwith several clusters keeping the main troughing off to our\nwest while others bring lower heights into the upper\nmidwest/Great Lakes. The ensembles do have a signal for at least\nsome precipitation and warmer than average temperatures. The\nECMWF EFI even has some unusual for the season CAPE in portions\nof our area. Thus, a few rumbles of thunder seem more likely\nduring this active pattern than any snow.",
